.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/combined-stream Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "combined-stream"
.PP
A stream that emits multiple other streams one after another\&.

.PP
\fBNB\fP Currently \fRcombined-stream\fP works with streams version 1 only\&. There is ongoing effort to switch this library to streams version 2\&. Any help is welcome\&. :) Meanwhile you can explore other libraries that provide streams2 support with more or less compatibility with \fRcombined-stream\fP\&.

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRcombined-stream2\fP: A drop-in streams2-compatible replacement for the combined-stream module\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRmultistream\fP: A stream that emits multiple other streams one after another\&.
.PP
.SH "Installation"
.PP
.PP
.nf
 bash
npm install combined\-stream
.fi
.PP
.SH "Usage"
.PP
Here is a simple example that shows how you can use combined-stream to combine two files into one:

.PP
.PP
.nf
 javascript
var CombinedStream = require('combined\-stream');
var fs = require('fs');

var combinedStream = CombinedStream\&.create();
combinedStream\&.append(fs\&.createReadStream('file1\&.txt'));
combinedStream\&.append(fs\&.createReadStream('file2\&.txt'));

combinedStream\&.pipe(fs\&.createWriteStream('combined\&.txt'));
.fi
.PP

.PP
While the example above works great, it will pause all source streams until they are needed\&. If you don't want that to happen, you can set \fRpauseStreams\fP to \fRfalse\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
 javascript
var CombinedStream = require('combined\-stream');
var fs = require('fs');

var combinedStream = CombinedStream\&.create({pauseStreams: false});
combinedStream\&.append(fs\&.createReadStream('file1\&.txt'));
combinedStream\&.append(fs\&.createReadStream('file2\&.txt'));

combinedStream\&.pipe(fs\&.createWriteStream('combined\&.txt'));
.fi
.PP

.PP
However, what if you don't have all the source streams yet, or you don't want to allocate the resources (file descriptors, memory, etc\&.) for them right away? Well, in that case you can simply provide a callback that supplies the stream by calling a \fRnext()\fP function:

.PP
.PP
.nf
 javascript
var CombinedStream = require('combined\-stream');
var fs = require('fs');

var combinedStream = CombinedStream\&.create();
combinedStream\&.append(function(next) {
  next(fs\&.createReadStream('file1\&.txt'));
});
combinedStream\&.append(function(next) {
  next(fs\&.createReadStream('file2\&.txt'));
});

combinedStream\&.pipe(fs\&.createWriteStream('combined\&.txt'));
.fi
.PP
.SH "API"
.PP
.SS "CombinedStream\&.create([options])"
Returns a new combined stream object\&. Available options are:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRmaxDataSize\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRpauseStreams\fP
.PP

.PP
The effect of those options is described below\&.
.SS "combinedStream\&.pauseStreams = \fRtrue\fP"
Whether to apply back pressure to the underlaying streams\&. If set to \fRfalse\fP, the underlaying streams will never be paused\&. If set to \fRtrue\fP, the underlaying streams will be paused right after being appended, as well as when \fRdelayedStream\&.pipe()\fP wants to throttle\&.
.SS "combinedStream\&.maxDataSize = \fR2 * 1024 * 1024\fP"
The maximum amount of bytes (or characters) to buffer for all source streams\&. If this value is exceeded, \fRcombinedStream\fP emits an \fR'error'\fP event\&.
.SS "combinedStream\&.dataSize = \fR0\fP"
The amount of bytes (or characters) currently buffered by \fRcombinedStream\fP\&.
.SS "combinedStream\&.append(stream)"
Appends the given \fRstream\fP to the combinedStream object\&. If \fRpauseStreams\fP is set to `true, this stream will also be paused right away\&.

.PP
\fRstreams\fP can also be a function that takes one parameter called \fRnext\fP\&. \fRnext\fP is a function that must be invoked in order to provide the \fRnext\fP stream, see example above\&.

.PP
Regardless of how the \fRstream\fP is appended, combined-stream always attaches an \fR'error'\fP listener to it, so you don't have to do that manually\&.

.PP
Special case: \fRstream\fP can also be a String or Buffer\&.
.SS "combinedStream\&.write(data)"
You should not call this, \fRcombinedStream\fP takes care of piping the appended streams into itself for you\&.
.SS "combinedStream\&.resume()"
Causes \fRcombinedStream\fP to start drain the streams it manages\&. The function is idempotent, and also emits a \fR'resume'\fP event each time which usually goes to the stream that is currently being drained\&.
.SS "combinedStream\&.pause();"
If \fRcombinedStream\&.pauseStreams\fP is set to \fRfalse\fP, this does nothing\&. Otherwise a \fR'pause'\fP event is emitted, this goes to the stream that is currently being drained, so you can use it to apply back pressure\&.
.SS "combinedStream\&.end();"
Sets \fRcombinedStream\&.writable\fP to false, emits an \fR'end'\fP event, and removes all streams from the queue\&.
.SS "combinedStream\&.destroy();"
Same as \fRcombinedStream\&.end()\fP, except it emits a \fR'close'\fP event instead of \fR'end'\fP\&.
